    "narrative": "Our strongest plays for today\u2019s slate are **Jonathan Aranda** (65.57) and **Lawrence Butler** (63.33). **Jonathan Aranda** draws a favorable matchup against Lugo, benefiting from a high xwOBA projection of .375 and a significant 12% platoon bump that elevates his floor against a mediocre xERA. Meanwhile, **Lawrence Butler** is poised for production against Roupp; despite the park penalty, the combination of a positive platoon split and favorable wind conditions makes him an essential start. **Bryson Stott** (62.84) also ranks highly, as his 13% platoon edge against Cavalli effectively neutralizes the pitcher's respectable 3.95 xERA.\n\nThe most difficult lineup decision today involved **Jung Hoo Lee** (51.70), who barely cleared our marginal threshold. While he benefits from a wind-aided environment, his score was dragged down by a double-digit platoon disadvantage against Springs. He remains a volatile play compared to the steadier output expected from **Geraldo Perdomo** (61.44), though both players carry elevated risk due to a 42% rain probability in their respective contests. We are keeping a close watch on the weather; if the radar worsens, we have depth ready, though our options are hampered by the continued absence of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Will Smith** on the IL.\n\nManagers should note that **Masyn Winn** (58.13) sits just inside our \"Strong\" start range, with his score buoyed by his sprint speed potential against a slower-delivering pitcher. While he and **Geraldo Perdomo** provide the stolen base upside we prioritize, the 42% rain risk necessitates monitoring the lineups and weather reports right up to first pitch. We are operating with a lean roster today, as the absence of **Taylor Ward** and **Tyler Freeman** due to scheduling, combined with our ongoing IL constraints, limits our ability to rotate in high-upside alternatives like **Daulton Varsho** or **Luis Arraez** to mitigate weather exposure.",
